steps.body:
This program sums the last `n` prices, and its loop runs one step too far, with `<=`:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

double window_sum(const std::vector<double>& prices, std::size_t n) {
    double sum = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = prices.size() - n; i <= prices.size(); ++i) {
        sum += prices[i];
    }
    return sum;
}

int main() {
    std::vector<double> prices = {101.25, 101.50, 101.75, 102.00, 101.90};
    std::cout << window_sum(prices, 3) << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

It prints 305.65, the right answer, and exits 0, here and on the Mac. Reading `prices[5]` is undefined behavior, and this time the bytes after the vector happened to add almost nothing. Tomorrow they won't.

**AddressSanitizer** (ASan) catches it. It's a compiler option that checks every memory access and surrounds every heap block, local array, and global with poisoned bytes, so touching one stops the program with a report. **UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer** (UBSan) checks for signed overflow, bad shifts, and null pointers. Build with both, plus `-g` so the report names lines, and run it in your Mac's terminal. This is its report, trimmed:

```text
$ clang++ -std=c++17 -g -fsanitize=address,undefined window.cpp -o window && ./window
==72740==ERROR: AddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow on address 0x604000000538 at pc 0x000102204a14 bp 0x00016dbf9e70 sp 0x00016dbf9e68
READ of size 8 at 0x604000000538 thread T0
    #0 0x000102204a10 in window_sum(std::__1::vector<double, std::__1::allocator<double>> const&, unsigned long) window.cpp:7
    #1 0x000102204ea8 in main window.cpp:14

0x604000000538 is located 0 bytes after 40-byte region [0x604000000510,0x604000000538)
allocated by thread T0 here:
    ...
    #8 0x000102204e7c in main window.cpp:13

SUMMARY: AddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow window.cpp:7 in window_sum(...)
```

Read it from the top. The kind of bug: `heap-buffer-overflow`. What happened: a `READ of size 8`, one double. Where: frame `#0` is the line that did it, and `#1` is its caller. Whose memory: 0 bytes past the end of a 40-byte block, the vector's 5 doubles, allocated at line 13. Other kinds read the same way. `heap-use-after-free` adds where the block was freed, such as inside a `push_back` that moved the vector, and `stack-buffer-overflow` names the local array.

UBSan prints one line and lets the program go on, unless you add `-fno-sanitize-recover=undefined`:

```text
notional.cpp:4:24: runtime error: signed integer overflow: 10125 * 300000 cannot be represented in type 'int'
```

ASan makes a program about 2 times slower and hungrier for memory, so it belongs in test and debug builds. On the Mac it ends the program with abort, status 134. On Linux, g++ takes the same flags and exits with 1, and MSVC has `/fsanitize=address`. Linux also has Valgrind, whose `valgrind ./a.out` finds the same memory bugs in an unchanged program, about 20 to 50 times slower, but doesn't run on Apple Silicon Macs. The sandbox here takes no flags, so sanitizer runs happen on your Mac.

